 on his farm.","They bred sheep for wool for generations.","They have bred high-quality cattle."],"subgroup":"base_past_past","vowel_pattern":"e-e-e"}},{"lemma":"bring","kind":"verbs","source":"tools/en/irregular-verbs/data/verbs.json","data":{"base":"bring","past_simple":"brought","past_participle":"brought","third_person_singular":"brings","pattern_group":"same_past_participle","level":"A1","definition":"To carry or convey something to a place.","examples":["She brings delicious snacks to work.","He brought a gift to the party.","They have brought their homework."],"subgroup":"ought_aught","vowel_pattern":"i-o-o"}},{"lemma":"bring about","kind":"verbs","source":"tools/en/verb-prep/data/verbs.json","data":{"is_phrasal":true,"separability":"Separable","particle":"about","transitivity":"Transitive","transitivity_code":"VT","prepositions":["about"],"pattern":"bring about [change] / bring [it] about","level":"B2","definition":"To cause something to happen or exist.","grammar_rule":"Separable phrasal verb. 'Bring it about' requires pronoun in middle.","examples":["New policy changes brought about significant improvement.","What brought about this sudden decision?"],"common_mistake":"❌ Bring about it. ➔ ✅ Bring it about.","synonyms":["cause","engender","produce"],"antonyms":["prevent"],"preposition_structure":"single","particle_count":1}},{"lemma":"bring up","kind":"verbs","source":"tools/en/verb-prep/data/verbs.json","data":{"is_phrasal":true,"separability":"Separable","particle":"up","transitivity":"Transitive","transitivity_code":"VT","prepositions":["up"],"pattern":"bring up [child/topic] / bring [topic / pronoun] up","level":"B1","definition":"To raise a child or to introduce a topic into conversation.","grammar_rule":"Separable phrasal verb. Pronoun object MUST split verb and particle ('bring it up').","examples":["She was brought up by her grandparents in Scotland.","Why did you bring up that uncomfortable subject at dinner?","It's a delicate topic, so don't bring